    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**Yes, it is a solid baseline offer, but it is not a top-of-band offer for an L6 PM in the Bay Area.** \n\n* **Base Salary ($235,000):** Sits near the middle of their stated range ($183,000 \u2013 $271,000). There is room to push toward the upper quartile.\n* **Target Bonus (20% / $47,000):** Standard for Google L6.\n* **Equity ($1,080,000 / 4 years):** This averages to **$270,000/year** on paper, but Google typically uses front-loaded vesting (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** in the US). \n  * **Year 1 & 2 Equity:** ~$356,400/yr\n  * **Year 3 Equity:** ~$237,600\n  * **Year 4 Equity:** ~$129,600\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($0):** Leaving a $0 sign-on bonus on the table when poaching an IC6 from Meta is standard initial positioning by Google recruiters, but it is the easiest lever to move.\n\n#### The Real Numbers Comparison:\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Google Offer (Year 1\u20132 Run Rate) | Google Offer (Year 3\u20134 Run Rate) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) |\n| **Equity (approx.)** | ~$250,000 | ~$356,400 (33% front-load) | ~$183,600 avg (22% / 12%) |\n| **Sign-on** | \u2014 | $0 | \u2014 |\n| **Total Comp (TC)** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400** | **~$465,600** *(excl. refreshes)* |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should you accept, negotiate, or walk away?\n\n**You should negotiate.**\n\nYou have the strongest possible leverage:\n1. **You were a passive candidate:** Google sought you out.\n2. **You are happy at Meta:** You have zero urgency to leave.\n3. **The Year 3/4 drop is real:** While Years 1 and 2 represent a jump over your Meta compensation, your Year 4 compensation drops below your current Meta floor unless refreshes cover the gap (which are performance-dependent and never guaranteed).\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to ask for and target numbers\n\nTo justify walking away from your current equity runway and organizational capital at Meta, target an updated package of **~$680k\u2013$720k Year 1 TC**:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter | Rationale / Leverage |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Stated band goes up to $271,000; matches/exceeds your current $240k base. |\n| **New-Hire RSUs** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,350,000** | Protects against the Year 3/4 vesting drop ($1.3M yields ~$429k/yr in Y1/Y2 and ~$286k in Y3). |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving unvested Meta RSUs ($200k vesting in next 12 months) and missing your upcoming Meta bonus cycle. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to structure the negotiation\n\nKeep it collaborative, highly anchored on total compensation, and emphasize that you want a clean incentive to make the jump.\n\n#### Suggested script for your recruiter call:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this together. I really enjoyed the team and the problem space, and I'd love to find a way to make this work.*\n>\n> *As we discussed early on, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and I\u2019m in a very strong position at Meta where my compensation trajectory is predictable. When I look at the current offer against what I'm walking away from\u2014including my unvested equity over the next 12 months and my current base salary\u2014this package doesn't quite provide the financial delta needed to make the transition.*\n>\n> *To make this an immediate 'yes' and close this out, I\u2019d be looking for:*\n> 1. *A **base salary of $255,000**, which reflects the senior scope and Bay Area band for L6.*\n> 2. *An equity grant of **$1,300,000 over 4 years**, which ensures strong alignment and bridges the back-half vesting schedule.*\n> 3. *A **sign-on bonus of $75,000\u2013$100,000** to offset walking away from my near-term Meta vesting schedule and bonus.*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to that level, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n#### Key Tactical Tips:\n* **Don't negotiate piecemeal:** Present all three requests (Base, Equity, Sign-on) together so they have room to trade off (e.g., if they push back on base, they can make it up in equity/sign-on).\n* **Confirm the vesting schedule in writing:** Ensure they confirm whether the GSU schedule is 33/33/22/12 or standard quarterly 25%, as this materially impacts your Year 1 cash flow.",
