     "content": "**Yes, you should accept this offer.** \n\nGetting Google to add a **$200,000 sign-on bonus** and bump base to match Meta on an L6 offer is an exceptional negotiation outcome. At Google L6, a $200k cash sign-on is at the very top of what the compensation committee approves without a competing written offer from another company.\n\nHere is the exact breakdown of why this is a massive win and what to verify before you sign.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers: Why this is a home run\n\nAssuming Google's standard front-loaded GSU vesting (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%):\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Bonus (Target)** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | $48,000 (20%) |\n| **Equity Vesting** | ~$250,000 | $356,400 (33%) | $356,400 (33%) |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | \u2014 | **$200,000** | \u2014 |\n| **Total Comp (TC)** | **~$526,000** | **~$844,400** | **~$644,400** |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n* **Year 1 Total Comp:** You will make **~$844,000**\u2014an increase of over **$318,000 (+60%)** compared to staying at Meta.\n* **Two-Year Runway:** Over your first 24 months, Google will pay you **~$1.49M** vs. Meta\u2019s **~$1.05M**. That is an extra **~$440,000 in your pocket** over two years.\n* **Downside Protection:** The $200k sign-on fully liquidates and de-risks the $200k unvested Meta RSUs you were worried about leaving behind.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Can (or should) you push further?\n\n**No.** When a Google recruiter returns with a six-figure cash concession and explicitly states it is their **\"best offer,\"** pushing again without an active, higher competing written offer from another company risks:\n1. Annoying the hiring manager/VP who had to expend political capital to get Comp Committee sign-off for that sign-on bonus.\n2. A flat refusal that puts you in an awkward \"take it or walk\" position.\n\nThey met your exact friction point (covering the $200k cliff) and matched your base. \n\n---\n\n### 3. A Few Details to Double-Check Before Signing\n\n1. **Sign-on Payout & Clawback Terms:** \n   * Verify whether the $200,000 is paid in full on your first or second paycheck (standard is first 30 days).\n   * Check the clawback period (typically 12 months prorated; make sure it isn't a 24-month lock).\n2. **Equity Vesting Schedule:** \n   * Confirm that the grant follows the standard front-loaded schedule (33% Year 1, 33% Year 2, 22% Year 3, 12% Year 4) rather than a flat 25% across 4 years.\n3. **Start Date & Bonus Timing:** \n   * Align your start date so you don't miss out on any imminent Meta bonus payouts if they are around the corner.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Accept Gracefully\n\nSend a positive email to lock it in:\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n>\n> *Thank you for working with the compensation committee to address the unvested equity gap. I appreciate the team\u2019s effort to put this package together.*\n>\n> *I\u2019m excited about the team and the opportunity, and I accept the offer of $240,000 base, 20% bonus target, $1,080,000 in GSUs, and the $200,000 sign-on bonus.* \n>\n> *Please send over the updated formal offer letter, and let\u2019s discuss the next steps on start dates and onboarding.\"*",
